# Chrome Print → PDF Production Pipeline

Single-file HTML (WordPress-compatible) → branded A4 PDF via Chrome headless print-to-pdf. Used for eBooks, field guides, teasers, posters.

## Render command
`cat a.html b.html > final.html` (merge parts), then:
`google-chrome --headless=new --no-sandbox --disable-gpu --no-pdf-header-footer --print-to-pdf=out.pdf file:///abs/path/final.html`
CSS `@page{size:A4;margin:16mm}` controls page size. Re-render AFTER every content edit; verify page count each time.

## Same-content-source pattern (DOCX + PDF from one block list)
When a deliverable must ship as an editable **DOCX** *and* a PDF (client bids, proposals,
reports), keep ONE content source and emit both — do not author the document twice:
- Content = python list of blocks `("H1"|"H2"|"H3"|"P"|"B"|"N"|"T"|"PB", value)` in separate
  modules; `mk_docx.py` (python-docx) writes the master, `mk_pdf.py` renders the same blocks to
  one HTML file with print CSS and calls Chrome.
- If no DOCX→PDF converter (`soffice`/`libreoffice`) is available, the HTML route below is the
  working substitute — no converter install needed. Re-render the HTML after any edit.
- Verified print CSS for documents: `@page{size:A4;margin:16mm 14mm}`,
  `body{font-family:Calibri,Arial;font-size:10.5pt;line-height:1.35}`,
  `table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:9pt}`,
  `th{background:#0B3C5D;color:#fff}`, `th,td{border:.5pt solid #999;padding:3px 5px;vertical-align:top}`.
- Chrome print-to-pdf renders no CSS running headers, so put the letterhead block at the top of
  page 1 and use the `position:fixed` footer pattern (below) for a repeating page footer.
- Verify each render: `pdfinfo out.pdf` for page count, `pdftotext out.pdf - | head -40` for text;
  clean up the intermediate HTML afterwards.

## Per-page footer (verified technique)
Chrome DOES repeat `position:fixed` elements on every printed page — no CSS paged-media margin boxes needed. Pattern:
- Base: `.pgfoot{display:none}` (screen), `@media print{.pgfoot{display:block;position:fixed;bottom:4mm;left:0;right:0;text-align:center;...}}`
- Footer sits in the bottom margin (4mm < 16mm) → zero overlap with content, no flow shift.
- Text via `text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.14em`.
- **Positive `bottom` values can collide with body text (verified 2026-09, markdown→PDF report).**
  Chrome lays the fixed element out against the *page content box*, not the paper edge: with
  `@page{margin:17mm 15mm}` + `bottom:3mm` the footer printed ON TOP of the last body line of
  pages 1 and 4. Working correction: widen the bottom margin AND push the footer into it —
  `@page{margin:17mm 15mm 20mm 15mm}` + `@media print{.pgfoot{bottom:-13mm}}` → footer sits ~7mm
  from the paper edge, no overlap, still repeats. After any margin/footer change, re-render and
  actually LOOK at page 1 and the LAST page PNG (`pdftoppm -png -r 80 -f N -l N`) — text extraction
  will never show you an overlap.
  Pitfall: fixed stops repeating if ANY ancestor has transform/filter/perspective (becomes absolute to that box). Keep the footer a plain descendant.
- **Negative `bottom` is also NOT stable (verified 2026-09-18):** after the body content changed (added a section, same 4-page count),
  `bottom:-13mm` drew the footer MID-PAGE, right between two bullets. Do not ship a long-lived doc on this trick.
- **Preferred, deterministic technique: CDP `Page.printToPDF` with `footerTemplate`.** Drop `.pgfoot` from the HTML entirely
  (or keep `.pgfoot{display:none}`), launch `google-chrome --headless=new --remote-debugging-port=9333 --user-data-dir=$(mktemp -d) file:///tmp/x.html`,
  poll `http://127.0.0.1:9333/json/list` for `type=="page"` (~1s; tolerate the first connection refusal — Chrome binds late), then over
  websocket-client send `{"id":1,"method":"Page.printToPDF","params":{printBackground:True,preferCSSPageSize:True,displayHeaderFooter:True,headerTemplate:"<div></div>",footerTemplate:FOOT,marginTop:0,marginBottom:0,marginLeft:0,marginRight:0}}`
  and `base64.b64decode(result["data"])` → file. `FOOT` MUST carry an explicit `font-size` on its outer div or Chrome renders it blank:
  `<div style="font-family:Calibri,Arial;font-size:8px;color:#5c686f;width:100%;padding:0 15mm;"><span>TITLE</span><span style="float:right;">Page <span class="pageNumber"></span> of <span class="totalPages"></span></span></div>`.
  Real page numbers for free, no negative-offset guessing. Needs `pip install websocket-client`; kill by `pkill -f remote-debugging-port=9333`.
**Verify with a letterspaced regex, not plain substring**: extracted text is `A A M I R  Z A M E E R` → `r'Z\s*A\s*M\s*E\s*E\s*R'`. Plain 'Aamir Zameer' matches body/bio/cover text and gives false page coverage.

## Inline SVG diagrams
- `<figure class="diag">` with `svg viewBox="0 0 940 N"`, `svg{width:100%;height:auto}` → scales to content width (~182mm); 1 viewBox unit ≈ 0.19mm.
- Print text legibility: bold titles ≥16 units, labels ≥12.5 units, captions as HTML under the SVG (12.5px) not inside it.
- **Unique marker IDs per figure** (`arrA`, `arrB`, `arrC`...) — duplicate `url(#id)` across multiple inline SVGs in one document collide.
- `figure.diag{break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid}` to stop splits.

## TOC page-number sync (after page-count shifts)
1. pypdf-extract text per page, search each section heading → actual start page.
2. Parse HTML TOC rows (`<span class="n">` + `<span class="t">` + `<span class="pg">NN</span>`), map title → new number, replace. HTML entities (`&amp;`) must be normalized before matching.
3. Re-render; count must stay stable (numbers are inline, no reflow).

## Teaser / extract-merge pipeline (pypdf)
- Extract: `PdfReader(out).pages[0:4]` → writer → save.
- 5th "poster" page = separately rendered standalone A4 HTML (own file, `@page{size:A4;margin:0}`) with big URL + QR.
- QR: generate PNG via `qrcode` lib, embed `img src="file:///tmp/qr.png"` — Chrome headless loads local file URLs fine.
- Merge with pypdf `writer.add_page(reader.pages[i])`, then verify page count + text per page.

## Large-HTML surgery pattern
Never hand-patch dozens of spots. Write a splice script (see references) + fragment files:
- Fragments as separate HTML files (`frag_fig_a.html` etc.), script reads + inserts at regex anchors with asserts (`count == 1`).
- Apply edits from LAST index to FIRST (reverse order) so offsets stay valid.
- **Idempotency marker**: check a comment string already present → skip, so re-runs don't double-apply (part files get re-written once, then guarded).

## "Blue shading" fix — pale tints, not blue hex (verified lesson)
User: *"remove the blue background shading, easy look easy reading like previous pdf, no other changes"*. Root cause: NO blue hex in source — pale mint/tint fills (`#F4FAF6`, `#FCFEFD`, `#DDF3E4`) have blue channel > red/green, so on screens they read as blue-gray boxes; a dark gradient page reads as heavy shading.
- Whitening = replace every pale fill with `#FFFFFF`; keep borders/accents (`#0E7A46`/`#BFE0CC` green family) untouched. "No other changes" means: do NOT touch layout/content/TOC/footer.
- **Patch BOTH the fragment sources AND the already-spliced copies** (part_a/part_b). Fragments get embedded at splice time — editing fragment files alone has ZERO visual effect on the merged PDF. Grep the merged parts for the fills and fix in place.
- **Duplicate CSS block trap**: a non-idempotent splice that ran twice inserts the personalization `<style>` twice. CSS cascade → the LAST block wins, so whitening the first block shows no effect and looks like a failed patch. Fix: scan all marker occurrences, drop duplicates, confirm `markers now: 1` before trusting output.
- **Marker matching in generated scripts**: prefer plain `.find()` substring over regex built from escaped strings — JSON file writes double backslashes (`\\*` becomes literal `\*` and never matches); a typo'd needle (missing `===`) silently returns -1. When a script run disagrees with an inline test, the script's own strings are the liar — test the exact bytes.
- **Pixel verification without vision** (no screenshot review needed): `pdftoppm -png -r 40 in.pdf pg`, then PIL-scan each PNG for bluish pixels (`blue - red > threshold`), report fraction per page. Source can be 100% green-family hex while blue-dominant pale fills still trip the scan — fix fills, re-scan to 0%.
- Scan the FULL pdf AND the teaser/poster: a dark gradient cover page only shows up on the teaser's extra page, so a full-PDF scan alone gives a false "clean".

## Markdown → branded client document set (SOW / questionnaire / runbook)

Jab source markdown ho aur output pe brand chahiye, HTML hand-author mat karo — ek render
pipeline likho (working copy: `~/ops/sap-readiness/render/render_docs.py` + `brand.json`
= colours, letterhead, badge classes, footer text):
- python-markdown (`tables`, `fenced_code`, `attr_list`) → per-doc HTML + print CSS →
  Chrome headless → `pdf/<DOC>.pdf`. Ek hi command se poora doc set re-render.
- **Token gate (hard fail):** `{{CLIENT}}`, `{{DATE}}`, `{{VERSION}}` substitute karo aur
  agar koi `{{...}}` bacha rahe to render ROOK do (non-zero exit). Adhoora client document
  bhejna sabse mehnga mistake hai.
- **Authoring notes ko client face se strip karo:** note `<!--internal-->…<!--/internal-->`
  me likho; `Client-facing` classification me block HTML se DELETE hota hai, `Internal` me
  rehta hai. Badge letterhead me visible rakho (kaun sa version kiske liye hai, saaf rahe).
- **Har render ke baad text-verify karo:** `pdftotext` + grep → leftover `{{` = 0,
  internal-note text = 0 (client docs), brand mentions > 0. Visual spot-check ke liye
  page-1 PNG (`pdftoppm`) dekho — cover hi pehla impression hai.
- **Cover-page markdown trap:** ek paragraph ke andar single newlines collapse ho jati hain —
  `Prepared for / Prepared by / Date / Validity` ko blank line se alag karo, warna chaar lines
  ek hi line me chipak jati hain (aur ye sirf render ke baad dikhta hai).
- Markdown source ko client naam ke liye ek fill-in line ke saath rakho (`Prepared for: ___`) —
  ek hi SOW har client ke liye reuse hota hai, copy fork nahi karni padti.
- **Report text-hygiene scan (client-face docs):** before sending, run
  `python3 ~/.hermes/skills/productivity/client-deliverable-patterns/scripts/ai_tell_scan.py <doc.md>`
  — asserts 0 em/en dashes, 0 curly quotes, 0 emoji, prints the
  non-ASCII set and AI-tell word hits (delve/crucial/seamless/"it is worth noting"/"not only"…).
  A plain-ASCII, sentence-case markdown source with real numbers reads as writer-typed; typographic
  quotes + em dashes are the giveaway that a model wrote it.
- Full recipe + script skeleton: `references/markdown-branded-doc-set.md`.

## PNG one-glance cards (same pipeline, `--screenshot`, no @page flow)
Fixed-canvas HTML → one crisp PNG image (one-glance tender cards, quote cards, status cards):
`google-chrome --headless=new --no-sandbox --disable-gpu --hide-scrollbars --force-device-scale-factor=2 --window-size=1080,1350 --screenshot=/abs/out.png file:///abs/card.html`
- Canvas comes from `--window-size`; `--force-device-scale-factor=2` → 2160×2700 PNG (WhatsApp/LinkedIn-crisp). CSS: `html,body{width:1080px;height:1350px;overflow:hidden}` + `@page{size:1080px 1350px;margin:0}`.
- Footer inside a fixed canvas = `position:absolute;bottom:0`; `position:fixed` is the print-PDF pattern and is wrong here.
- **Fixed height ⇒ dead space is the #1 defect.** After the first render, actually LOOK at the PNG (`vision_analyze`): a gap between the last block and the bottom-anchored footer means the canvas under-fills. Fix by ADDING a real value section (e.g. a 3-tile "how we can work together" strip), not by stretching margins.
- **Dark + light twin variants:** ONE markup, swap only the `:root` palette block (bg/surface/text/accent) → identical layout geometry, so edits apply to both; patch both files in a single python replace pass, then re-render and re-verify both.
- Confirm the scale factor landed by asserting PNG size (PIL → `(2160, 2700)`).
- Card content rules for this user: pure English, **no faces/living beings, no emoji**, brand + city in footer, and **never invent contact numbers/emails/logos** — omit them and ask for the real ones.
- Starter card: skill `govt-tender-bid-ops` → `templates/one-glance-tender-card.html`.
- Card-build path used in practice: `~/clients/az-consultants/tender-cards/fm_tender_card_{dark,light}.html`
  → `fm_tender_card_{dark,light}.png` (the older `~/hmw_bid/graphics/…` set is FROZEN — that brand is
  retired for client-facing work).
- **A rebrand is not a footer swap.** When the client-facing brand changes, the footer, the services /
  "what we bring" strip, the letterhead monogram and the contact block ALL have to be rebuilt in the
  target brand's own words — pull the service lines and contact details off the target brand's live
  site, never recycle the previous brand's capability list. Before delivering, grep the card HTML for
  every retired brand string (HMW / Air Controls / Triatek / Sanad) and assert zero hits; also keep the
  no-faces / no-emoji rule. Correction that came with figures (e.g. bid-validity days, a securities
  detail) doubles as legitimate content for the dead-space filler strip.

## Pitfalls & gotchas
- pdftoppm output naming: prefix `pg-0` yields `pg-0-01.png` (dash inserted before page num) — rename or use exact `pg-0` + loop.
- uv-created venvs have NO `pip` binary: use `uv pip install --python /tmp/venv/bin/python <pkgs>`.
- Verification of text on printed pages: pypdf `extract_text()` order is not visual order — search distinctive tokens, don't assume layout position.
- Truncation guard: long file writes (SVG/fragments/scripts) can truncate mid-content when built in one shot — write each fragment as its own file via separate calls.

